{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2160-10-25 02:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, I'm Dr. Lee. You're here because of chest pain and passing out. Can you tell me what happened?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2160-10-25 02:47:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yeah, I was drinking and saw a fight. Then I got this chest pain, just like my old heart attacks. It went away for a bit, but came back worse. I passed out, hit my head."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2160-10-25 02:48:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. EMS said your EKG looked concerning, but when we checked, it was mostly the same as before. No new changes except for some Q waves in V5–V6."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2160-10-25 02:49:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So is it my heart again? Feels just like last time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2160-10-25 02:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We checked your troponin, which is a blood test for heart damage. It was negative. We’ll repeat it in a few hours to be sure, but right now, it doesn’t look like a new heart attack."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2160-10-25 02:51:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But I passed out. That’s not normal, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2160-10-25 02:52:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, it’s not. We did a CT scan of your head and neck because you hit your head. There’s a scalp hematoma, but no skull fracture or brain bleed. Your spine looks stable too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2160-10-25 02:53:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So why did I pass out?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2160-10-25 02:54:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Given you were drinking and your symptoms started during stress, it’s likely the alcohol played a big role. Your heart rhythm and tests don’t show a dangerous cause."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2160-10-25 03:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We also did a chest X-ray. It shows some vascular congestion, which can be a sign of heart strain, but no pneumonia or collapsed lung."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2160-10-25 03:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had chest pain every other day, but it’s been less since I started taking my meds again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2160-10-25 03:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s good. Your echo from earlier this year showed your heart function is reduced, about 36% ejection fraction. You’ve had stents, but some areas are permanently damaged."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2160-10-25 04:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your repeat troponin came back negative again. That’s reassuring. It’s very unlikely you had a heart attack this time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2160-10-25 04:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel okay now. But my brother just overdosed. My sister’s coming to get me. I need to go."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2160-10-25 04:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand, but I strongly recommend you stay. We haven’t optimized your heart meds yet, and you’re still at risk. You haven’t had your beta-blocker, aspirin, or statin since arriving."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2160-10-25 04:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I know, but I can’t stay. I’ll call my PCP and get my meds refilled."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2160-10-25 04:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. Just know you’re leaving against medical advice. Your chest pain isn’t from a new heart attack, but your heart disease is serious. Please follow up soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2160-10-25 04:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks for checking me out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2160-10-25 05:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Before you go, your labs show your kidney and blood counts are normal. Your CPK was a bit high last night, but it’s coming down—likely from the fall or stress, not heart damage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2160-10-25 05:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So no heart damage, no brain bleed. Just the alcohol and stress?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2160-10-25 05:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s the most likely explanation. But your heart disease is real and needs ongoing care. Please don’t stop your medications again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2160-10-25 05:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I won’t. I’ve been trying to stay clean. No heroin, no cocaine. Just had a few drinks this time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2160-10-25 05:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a good start. If you’re willing, your PCP can help with addiction support too. This hospitalization could’ve been avoided with better control."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2160-10-25 05:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll think about it. Just gotta handle this family stuff first."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2160-10-25 05:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Understood. Here’s a note for your PCP summarizing everything. Please follow up within a week."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2160-10-25 05:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. I’ll call them tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2160-10-25 05:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re alert and walking fine. If anything changes—chest pain, dizziness—come back right away."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2160-10-25 05:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks, doc."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2160-10-25 02:46:00",
    "admission_end": "2160-10-25 09:45:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted with chest pain and a syncopal event after witnessing a fight and consuming alcohol. He reported symptoms similar to prior cardiac events, with substernal chest pain radiating to the left shoulder and transient loss of consciousness, resulting in a head laceration. Initial EKG showed new Q waves in V5–V6 but no acute ST changes, and troponins were negative on two occasions. CT head and spine were unremarkable except for a scalp hematoma. Chest X-ray revealed vascular congestion suggestive of heart strain. The syncopal episode was attributed to acute alcohol intoxication given the negative cardiac workup. The patient left against medical advice before receiving his cardiac medications, citing a family emergency. He planned to follow up with his PCP for medication management and ongoing care.",
    "problems": [
      "Other chest pain",
      "Coronary atherosclerosis of native coronary artery",
      "Opioid abuse, unspecified",
      "Personal history of tobacco use"
    ]
  }
}
